AUDIO: 'Hi, I\'m Richard from Electric Classic Cars, and on this week\'s episode, sponsored by Mouser Electronics and Microchip, it\'s all about the gorgeous Karmann Ghia, or more specifically, about our new bolt-in electric conversion kit for the Karmann Ghia.'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Let\'s get into it. Now, for regular viewers of the channel, you\'ll know this is the most important build we\'ve ever done, because it\'s my'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wife\'s Karmann Ghia. But, it\'s taken a while, because what we\'ve done is we\'ve designed a repeatable bolt-in kit around this. We could have just like dove straight into it, converted it, and da-da, there you go,'

[00:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and got loads of brownie points, but it\'s been delayed because we\'ve got to have a repeatable kit built around this Karmann Ghia. And this, if you ask me, is the most beautiful Volkswagen they ever produced. I love my Beetles and'

[00:51] [scene:kit]
VISUAL: Overhead shot of a VW Beetle on a lift with electric conversion components laid out. Text overlays "ELECTRIC VW BEETLE" and "BOLT IN KIT".
AUDIO: 'buses, but the Karmann Ghia, I mean, just look at that. Look at the lines of it. But, the one problem this car always has, or had, it never went as good as it looked, but we have sorted that out.'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, we\'ve been converting VW Beetles for 10 years. In fact, the very first car I ever converted was a 1966 Beetle. So, initially, I was thinking we\'ll probably just use our very well-developed'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Volkswagen Beetle kit. Click on the link above if you want to see more information on that. Because, if you have a look inside, if you come closer, Tim, have a look. That, for all you Volkswagen guys, that is essentially a Beetle floor pan in there. And if you'

[01:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'come around the front, anybody that knows their Beetles, if you have a look in there, this just looks like the same as a Beetle front end. You\'ve got your fuel tank here, you\'ve got your beam on the front. And effectively,'

[01:43] [scene:interior]
VISUAL: Man sitting in the rear of a Karmann Ghia, pointing to the empty luggage area.
AUDIO: 'well, I\'d say 90% of the basis of the car is Volkswagen Beetle, but there was a lot uh more um than we thought, a lot of very important differences, which'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'meant we had to pretty much change most of the Beetle kit to make it fit in the Karmann Ghia. So, in the front we effectively copied the Beetle kit because the fuel tank area is pretty much the same really.'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Obviously, the low voltage loom and the high voltage loom had to completely change because of lengths and routings if you like. But, the main biggest change we had to do is in the rear battery pack because in a Beetle behind the back seat'

[02:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you\'ve got quite a high luggage area where the the battery box in the rear can kind of like sit vertically. But, in the Karmann Ghia no such luck. So, we had to essentially take that Beetle'

[02:35] [scene:interior]
VISUAL: Man pointing to the empty luggage area of a Karmann Ghia.
AUDIO: 'battery pack and what I thought would be really simple just put it flat so that we can fit it in here. That was yeah, that was the idea. But, we had to do quite a lot of changes to make it fit in here because the in'

[02:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Beetle where the high voltage and low voltage plugs come out was then not feasible. So, we ended up making a complete different new rear battery pack that went in here. But, in the rear and the'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the motor side of things that\'s again pretty much the same. So, in the rear here as I say it\'s pretty much a Beetle kit cradle with the Tesla small drive unit rear'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that goes in here. Excuse this by the way, this is a bit of R&amp;D we\'re doing with a different motor at the moment. But, yeah normally what would sit in there is that Beetle cradle and Tesla motor. But, you\'ll notice in the Karmann Ghia you\'ve got'

[03:27] [scene:exterior]
VISUAL: Man standing at the rear of a Karmann Ghia with the boot open, gesturing to the empty space.
AUDIO: 'much wider rear arches or haunches if you like that we can put stuff into. And also yeah, the other thing that\'s different we\'ve got actually what\'s the same as we\'ve got this charger here. But, what\'s'

[03:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different is we can put the header tanks for the coolant system for the motor and the battery pack in here cuz we just got more space in this area here. The other thing is behind me here in this rear'

[03:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wheel arch, we can put a big high voltage junction box, which also acts as the charge socket, and I\'ll explain why in a minute. So, in our niche world of EV'

[04:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conversions, this is the latest and greatest technology, if you like. But, in the OEM automotive world, there\'s a company called Microchip, who are on the cutting edge of EV technology. Now, before we continue, I just want to'

[04:18] [scene:product]
VISUAL: Rear view of a grey Jaguar I-Pace parked on a street.
AUDIO: 'say a massive thank you to today\'s episode sponsor, Microchip Technology. Now, it might be a name that you recognize, but their innovative technologies are the building blocks that you will find in the electronics components in modern EVs like this'

[04:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Jaguar I-Pace here and the Renault Zoe over there. Now, modern EVs are packed with electronics that manage everything from the battery charging, and motor control, to safety systems, and vehicle'

[04:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'communications. And Microchip develops the semiconductors and control technologies that help make all of that happen, making electric vehicles more efficient and more reliable and easier to develop.'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'As enthusiasts, we tend to focus on things like the motors and the batteries, but really it\'s the electronics behind the scenes that is helping to make an EV drive so well and charge efficiently and keep everything working safely together. Now, if you\'d'

[05:10] [scene:workshop]
VISUAL: Man standing next to a large metal frame holding electric car components, with a Karmann Ghia in the background.
AUDIO: 'like to find out more about this technology that\'s helping shape the future of EVs, go to the links in the description, and there\'s plenty more information you can find there. Now, on that note, back to the episode. So, we\'ve got the complete common gear kit'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, all mounted onto our universal test stand, so I can take you for a spin in a minute. But, before I do, I\'m going to give you a little bit of a tour around the kit itself. So, we\'ll start'

[05:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the big boxes. That\'s a 42 kW hour battery pack split between the front box and the rear box. As I already mentioned, this front box here, this mounts into that space there where the fuel tank used to be. And the rear box'

[05:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here, that mounts underneath the rear parcel shelf, if you like, where the old luggage space used to be in there. And then we\'ve got in the rear space the charger, the motor'

[06:01] [scene:components]
VISUAL: Man standing behind a table displaying various metal brackets and pedals.
AUDIO: 'down there you probably can\'t see, and the high voltage junction box. All that lot there goes in there. But that\'s not all, because obviously we\'ve got the high voltage wiring loom, the low voltage wiring loom, and all the other bits and pieces'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like accelerator pedal, you might need that to make it go, header tanks and bracketry here that make the installation of our bolt-in kit super simple. Now, before I'

[06:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fire up that kit over there and take it for a spin, I just want to spend a little bit of a moment on showing you the depth that we go to as far as developing our kits are concerned, because we cover everything, including'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cable management. For instance, there\'s no having to cable tie stuff up. The looms are all done, the lengths are done, the plugs are on. It is all done. But just to show you the detail, I mean,'

[06:52] [scene:installation]
VISUAL: Close-up of a hand holding a metal plate against the underside of a car chassis.
AUDIO: 'the cables that come through from the rear to the front go through the center spine, the tunnel, if you like. And when they come out, that is a plate that goes onto the inspection plate at the front, just between the beams on the front. And then'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the cables go through there, and then they do a little bit of a back swan neck, if you like. There, this in fact goes on the front beam, the top of the beam, and the cables then go through'

[07:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that. So, if you imagine that\'s there, cables come through that, and then go up through here. And then just on the top then is this, which essentially sits where the old fuel tank is and'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'separates the, you know, outside from the inside. And then the cables come up through here and here. And this can be flipped over whether or not it\'s left-hand drive or right-hand drive. Uh and then underneath here is where'

[07:46] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of a black control unit with a rotary knob, installed in the car's interior.
AUDIO: 'your DC-to-DC converter goes. And on top is where the battery pack goes. So, we\'ve got all these plates which have been designed for cable management. This goes on the rear. This goes underneath where the old gearbox used to go.'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Um and this plate here, for instance, is another differentiated between the Beetle kit and the Karmann Ghia kit. Because with the Karmann Ghia, they there\'s a a heater knob where the main control box can\'t really sit in the same'

[08:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'place as the Beetle. So, this little conversion plate allows us to use our Beetle control box, but just move it slightly. So, all these things here shouldn\'t be underestimated in a bolting'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kit because this makes life so much easier when you\'re installing a kit. &gt;&gt; [snorts] &gt;&gt; Now, the problem is whenever you develop a bolting kit, and you know, you build them and ship them out uh all around the'

[08:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of a control panel with buttons labeled "Ignition", "Brake Light", "Rev Light", "Fault Light", "Indicators", "Stop Switch", and a rotary knob for drive selection.
AUDIO: 'world. In fact, this one is going out to our partners in Colorado. Shout out to Nick, Classic EV Conversions out there. So, whenever you do that, it\'s quite difficult to figure out how you\'re going to test these things to'

[08:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make sure they work before they go in a crate and travel around the world. And that\'s why we\'ve built our universal test bench. And this test bench here simulates effectively a car. So, you got brake pedal, accelerator. You got'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'direction knobs. We\'ve got um test there so we can see if the reverse lights coming on, if the brake lights coming on. Basically, this is a car without the wheels on it. Oh, it has got wheels. With mind their own wheels, as we found'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'out. &gt;&gt; it doesn\'t handle as well as most cars, does it? &gt;&gt; No, it handles about as well as a Tim\'s Toyota FJ Jeep thing. Um but this is our universal test bench where we test Land Rover kits, Mini kits, Porsche 911 kits, VW kits, all of'

[09:31] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Man pointing to a high voltage disconnect switch on the test bench.
AUDIO: 'our kits gets tested on here for whether or not they go forward, backwards, any faults, issues. We simulate problems as well, charging. We put the'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'charging systems through their paces well, cooling systems. Everything gets tested on here before they go into a crate and then get shipped out. Right, enough waffle. Let\'s take you guys for a spin, but the first thing I'

[09:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got to do is put in the service disconnect. There we go. That\'s in, so that means that everything can become alive now, so that\'s the high voltage service disconnect or safety disconnect as some people call it. So'

[10:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'now that\'s in, I can switch the system on and take you for a spin. So we got seat out here. I\'ve got my throttle down there and if I switch the ignition on, if you want to'

[10:24] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of the Karmann Ghia's original dashboard gauges.
AUDIO: 'Do you want to film me put the ignition on, Tim? This switch here. Go on, Tim. Ready? Go. There we go. You\'ll hear as the contactors have now closed, so the system\'s now live. So I\'ve got my'

[10:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'display here and this is the control um console, if you like, that goes in the center and normally gets bolted in where the old gear stick used to go. So that\'s got you all the information in from'

[10:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'state of charge to how many amps, etc. That means you can keep your original dial on the Karmann Ghia and Beetles because all that\'s really on there anyway is the speedo and that\'s mechanically driven off one of the front'

[11:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wheels. So you just bolt that in, that\'s got all the information you need and also here we\'ve got our direction knob and if I put it into D for drive, there we go. And if Tim wants to come'

[11:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'around the side of me here now and if you want to zoom in down there on that drive shaft down there, you\'ll see we should be spinning forwards if I give it a little bit of throttle, give it some beans. There we go.'

[11:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So that\'s going forwards and if I put it into reverse, there we go. That\'s in reverse and it should now go into reverse. Here we go. And if you come up here, Tim, you\'ll see the reverse light has'

[11:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'come on. Oops, sorry. Reverse light\'s now on. And if I Now the brake light\'s on, I can put it into neutral. Reverse light goes out. So, all these things we can expectantly simulate on the car. And now we\'ve been'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'for a spin. You know, that\'s a long old journey. It\'s time to charge up, isn\'t it? Yeah, we\'ve done miles, haven\'t we? Knackered. Time to have a burger and charge up.'

[12:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, this is our high voltage junction box I mentioned earlier. This sits in the rear left wing of the Karmann Ghia and it\'s also the charging socket. Now, why is the charging socket here and not'

[12:19] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Man demonstrating plugging a charging cable into the test bench.
AUDIO: 'on the outside of the car? I\'ll give you a second to shout at the screen. It is because there\'s no external fuel flap on an early \'60s Karmann Ghia. Late Later ones, \'70s ones, there are.'

[12:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Um it\'s the same with the Beetle. Um so, there\'s no external fuel filler flap. You used to have to lift up the front um of the uh bonnet, if you like, and then fill up with fuel. So, we\'ve got um high'

[12:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'voltage junction box at the back with various different plates that can go on here from type one, type one CCS, type two, type two CCS, etc. So, this is a um a modular uh plate that goes on here.'

[12:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, this customer has got type one. So, if I do that and plug this in, so this is um mainly for the US market. Uh And if I plug that in there and go like that, you\'ll hear'

[13:12] [scene:components]
VISUAL: Man kneeling next to the test bench, showing cooling system components.
AUDIO: 'should hear Here you go. There\'s a contactor\'s firing up and it\'s now charging. Now, why is it going this way and not, you know, vertically? Oh, there you go. So, the fan of the charger\'s just come'

[13:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on. It\'s now charging up. It\'s because when you got the boot open this way, the cable wants to come out this way rather than down. So, we turn that through 90°. So, there we go. It\'s now charging up. It\'s worth mentioning cooling-wise,'

[13:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ve actually started to strip this down ready to go out and get shipped off this week. So, we\'ve already done the coolant um tests, but essentially what you\'ve got, you\'ve got radiators here for the motor and the battery pack, all the'

[13:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coolant lines here which haven\'t got them in the uh box yet. Then you\'ve got your header tank that goes up there onto This is the battery pump, the motor pumps the other side, but all this has already been tested, so I can\'t show you this today'

[14:03] [scene:components]
VISUAL: Man pointing to various electronic control units mounted on the test bench.
AUDIO: 'because it\'s already got to stripped down. Another thing I just forgot to mention as well is the low voltage power distribution package is here. So, you\'ve got the 12-V battery there, uh midi fuses, uh 12-V fuses and relays and'

[14:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stuff there. So, all this uh sits underneath the back seat. And if you follow me around here, what sits in uh the other space underneath the back seat is all the controllers. So, we have traction'

[14:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control module, um your motor controller, and your VCU sitting underneath the other side of the back seat. Now, all of our kits uh and conversions are built to certain international'

[14:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standard called ECE R100 Rev 1, which means it it\'s compliant with things like uh there\'s isolation monitoring in there to make sure there\'s no high voltage leak into the chassis. And uh I\'m not going to go through the details now, but'

[14:55] [scene:components]
VISUAL: Man gesturing towards the wiring and components on the test bench.
AUDIO: 'there\'s one thing I do want to show you, which is um little thing here. Now, this for instance is a buzzer, so that if you\'ve left the vehicle in gear and you\'ve opened up the'

[15:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'door, which is easily done in electric car because, you know, they\'re quiet and you don\'t know you\'ve done it. Uh so, there\'s a little buzzer uh that sounds, that\'s part of R100. But this here, this is an important one. This is an inertia switch. So, if there\'s an impact, you\'re'

[15:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'involved in a uh um a a crash or somebody\'s smashed into you or for instance with my VW bus that got crashed last year, this is the thing that will detect the G forces of that crash and shut everything'

[15:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'off. Now, I want to give you a little bit of a display of this now. So, Tim, can you see that the vehicle is on over there? So, you can see that dashboard there. &gt;&gt; lit up. &gt;&gt; And the fault light over there, the fault light is off.'

[15:45] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Close-up of the test bench control panel showing the "Fault Light" illuminated red.
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Fault light is off. &gt;&gt; Okay. Now, if I bring my whacky stick, I\'m going to simulate a crash hopefully with enough G force that it\'ll trigger. So, if I whack this thing now,'

[15:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m not sure if that worked or if it was big enough crash, but yeah, I heard the contactors. The contactor\'s opened and the whole of high voltage has been switched off and now'

[16:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there\'s a fault light, basically a you cannot drive the car light that is now come on because now you\'ve been involved in an accident. Did that hurt, Tim? &gt;&gt; Didn\'t feel a thing.'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Well, where there\'s a blame, there\'s a claim. Uh so, that is one of the additional safety features, if you like, that we have on our conversions and kits that is part of the standards that we build to. So, there we go. You had a little tour'

